Hi Robin,

We are working on a manuscript covering this issue right now. For the moment, I would consider a couple of options:
In his comparative analysis, Mike looked at overlap estimates between home ranges estimated from the first half of the data and from the second half of the data, and applied a subjective overlap threshold to classify resident and non-resident: https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/ecm.1344
New for the upcoming manuscript, I've implemented a ranging area cluster() function that will objectively classify area estimates into two groups—small (resident) and large (non-resident)—using a hierarchical mixture model.
You might also consider applying a threshold DOF['area'] value. One-off migrations and dispersals will have a DOF['area'] of around 1, which is much less than the number of times an ungulate would tend to cross its home range over an extended period of time.
I would try sorting the output variogram comparisons (empirical versus theoretical) by one of these statistics (overlap, P(resident), DOF) and skimming through them to see which aligns more with your sense of the data.
In addition to variograms, I also find it useful to color the scatter plots by time, like

COL <- color(DATA,by='time')
plot(DATA,col=COL)

Best,
Chris

